; apps/cword/welcome.wtx - the shipped WELCOME.RTF's source (SPEC.md 70.12.3).
; Same markup and the same parser as apps/word/welcome.wtx (tools/os88doc.py
; owns parse_line; tools/os88rtf.py turns the result into RTF instead of .DOC).
;
; WHY THIS IS A SECOND SOURCE AND NOT THE SAME FILE. cword's RTF reader carries
; the text and its CHARACTER formatting; every paragraph arrives in the default
; format, which apps/cword/cwrtfio.c's ovl_put() says in as many words. So the
; paragraph directives that make the .DOC demonstrate centring, indents and
; double spacing would be silent here - and the sentences NAMING them would be
; claims the screen does not keep, which is the one thing a shipped sample
; document must never be (SPEC.md 47 is the same argument about greying).
; Two spans go the same way: \ulw and \uldb both fold into one underline in
; cword's model and there is no small caps at all, so those three are named in
; the text and not worn by it.
;
; What is left is the same document: the same greeting, the same demonstration
; that formatting survived a save and a load, the same key list, the same
; credit.
{b}{u}WELCOME TO MICROSOFT WORD{/u}{/b}
Version 1.1a for os8088, written in C

This document is an RTF file: the {b}bold{/b}, the {i}italics{/i} and the {u}underline{/u} on this page were saved into it and are being rendered back by the same engine you are typing into.

{b}What works{/b}
Type anywhere; select with the mouse, or press F8 and extend with the arrow keys. Character formats live on the ribbon, in Format Character, and on the Ctrl keys: {b}bold{/b} (Ctrl-B), {i}italic{/i}, {b}{i}both together{/b}{/i} and {u}underline{/u} (Ctrl-U). Word underline (Ctrl-W), double underline (Ctrl-D) and small caps (Ctrl-K) are drawn in the window too - this FILE cannot carry them, because cword's RTF folds every underline into one and has no small caps.

Paragraph formats - alignment, line spacing, space before and the three indents - come from the ruler, the Ctrl keys and Format Paragraph. Set them and the page follows; they last as long as the document is open, and what this file carries from one session to the next is the text and its character formatting.

{b}A few keys{/b}
Ctrl-F        Search
F4 / Shift-F4 Repeat the search down / up
F5            Go To a page
Ins           Overtype (the status bar's OVR lamp)
F8            Extend the selection (EXT)
Shift-F12     Save
F12           Save As
Alt-BkSp      Undo

View > Draft wraps the text to the window; View > Page shows the sheet it will print on. The Font list is built from the system disk's FONTS folder and sets the document in a proportional face. The menus, ribbon, ruler, status line and dialogs follow the Opus source release preserved by the Computer History Museum.
Save your own documents into the DOCS folder with Save As.
